{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2601.17191, which Global Majority countries participated in drafting the 2024 Council of Europe Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence, and what does the Convention allow for non-member countries?", "answer": "Argentina, Costa Rica, Mexico, Peru, and Uruguay participated in drafting. The Convention allows observer and non-CoE member countries to sign, as with a prior data protection treaty.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "Argentina, Costa Rica, Mexico, Peru, and Uruguay participated in the negotiation and drafting of the Convention (Council of Europe, 2024a; Council of Europe, 2024b)."}, {"page": 15, "quote": "Observer and non-CoE member countries may sign this Convention just like eight non-European countries signed the Convention for the Protection of Individuals with regard to Automatic Processing of Personal Data (Council of Europe, n.d.)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2601.17191, what are the four working groups of the Commonwealth Artificial Intelligence Consortium (CAIC) and which country leads the Consortium?", "answer": "The four working groups are research and innovation, capacity building, data and infrastructure, and policy development. Rwanda leads the Consortium.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "The Consortium AI Ecosystem is divided into four working groups to address research and innovation, capacity building, data and infrastructure, and policy development. CAIC initiatives include the Commonwealth AI Academy, Commonwealth AI incubator, AI entrepreneurship Make-a-Thon, and Simplilearn E-learning to boost AI talents, skills, and innovation growth (CAIC, 2024)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2601.17191, which Global Majority countries are OECD members and which adhere to the OECD AI Principles without being members?", "answer": "Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, and Mexico are OECD members. Argentina, Brazil, Egypt, Peru, and Uruguay adhere to the OECD AI Principles as non-members.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, and Mexico are OECD members from the Global Majority. Some Global Majority countries are non-OECD members but adhere to the OECD AI principles, including Argentina, Brazil, Egypt, Peru, and Uruguay (OECD, 2019)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2601.17191, what is the main purpose of the Africa Declaration on Artificial Intelligence, and how many African Union member states endorsed it?", "answer": "The Africa Declaration aims to foster ethical, inclusive, and sustainable AI development in Africa. It was endorsed by 49 out of 55 African Union member states.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "Notably, the Summit produced the Africa Declaration on Artificial Intelligence, a landmark agreement endorsed by 49 out of 55 African Union member states, the African Union secretariat, and Smart Africa (C4IR, 2025). It aims to foster ethical, inclusive, and sustainable AI development across the continent while positioning Africa as a global leader in AI governance."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2601.17191, which organization launched a center in South Africa in 2024 to support AI policy research and development?", "answer": "The Global Center on AI Governance was launched in South Africa in 2024 to support AI policy research and development.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "For example, the Global Center on AI Governance 9 was launched in 2024 in South Africa and has been influential in conducting empirical policy research and contributing towards AI policy development throughout the African continent."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2601.17191, which summit led to the creation of a working group proposing an Intergovernmental Council on Artificial Intelligence for Latin America?", "answer": "The Ministerial and High Authorities Summit on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence in Latin America and the Caribbean, hosted by Chile in October 2023, led to this working group.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "In October 2023, Chile hosted the Ministerial and High Authorities Summit on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Latin America and the Caribbean, which led to the Santiago Declaration on AI Ethics (Santiago Declaration, 2023; Maffioli, 2023). 8 The Summit also led to the creation of a Chile-led working group with a proposition to create an Intergovernmental Council on Artificial Intelligence for Latin America (Santiago Declaration, 2023; Maffioli, 2023)."}]}
]}
